window.docContents[51].resolve({"/Basic-Types/Maps-and-Sets/#HashMap":{"contents":"The declarations in this section should be imported using import Std.HashMap.\n\nHash maps.This is a simple separate-chaining hash table. The data of the hash map consists of a cached size\nand an array of buckets, where each bucket is a linked list of key-value pairs. The number of buckets\nis always a power of two. The hash map doubles its size upon inserting an element such that the\nnumber of elements is more than 75% of the number of buckets.The hash table is backed by an Array. Users should make sure that the hash map is used linearly to\navoid expensive copies.The hash map uses == (provided by the BEq typeclass) to compare keys and hash (provided by\nthe Hashable typeclass) to hash them. To ensure that the operations behave as expected, ==\nshould be an equivalence relation and a == b should imply hash a = hash b (see also the\nEquivBEq and LawfulHashable typeclasses). Both of these conditions are automatic if the BEq\ninstance is lawful, i.e., if a == b implies a = b.These hash maps contain a bundled well-formedness invariant, which means that they cannot\nbe used in nested inductive types. For these use cases, Std.Data.HashMap.Raw and\nStd.Data.HashMap.Raw.WF unbundle the invariant from the hash map. When in doubt, prefer\nHashMap over HashMap.Raw.Dependent hash maps, in which keys may occur in their values' types, are available as\nStd.Data.DHashMap.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Basic Types\u0009Maps and Sets","header":"21.19.2. Hash Maps","id":"/Basic-Types/Maps-and-Sets/#HashMap"},"/releases/v4.16.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___16___0-_LPAR_2025-02-03_RPAR_--Highlights":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.16.0 (2025-02-03)","header":"Highlights","id":"/releases/v4.16.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___16___0-_LPAR_2025-02-03_RPAR_--Highlights"},"/releases/v4.31.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___31___0-_LPAR_2026-06-13_RPAR_--Highlights":{"contents":"Lean 4.31.0 is a consolidation-heavy release: alongside a handful of new user-facing features — do blocks elaboration, Lake built-in linting, and richer editor hovers — it lands a large, coordinated effort to make definitional-equality checking properly respect transparency levels, a faster and reimplemented mvcgen', significant development in libraries including HTTP, and broad performance work including an LLVM 22 upgrade.\n\nThis highlights section was contributed by Juanjo Madrigal.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.31.0 (2026-06-13)","header":"Highlights","id":"/releases/v4.31.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___31___0-_LPAR_2026-06-13_RPAR_--Highlights"},"/releases/v4.35.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___35___0-_LPAR_2026-10-12_RPAR_--Highlights--Tactic-Changes--A-Redesigned--rwa":{"contents":"rwa used to be a plain macro for rw ...; assumption, so the assumption step could end up closing some unrelated goal.\n#14937 changes it so that it only works on the goal it was called on, and on the side goals created by the rewrite: rwa [rules] rewrites the goal and closes it with an assumption, while rwa [rules] at h rewrites h and then closes the main goal using h itself, rather than any matching assumption.\nWhen the rewrite alone already closes the goal, it suggests using rw instead:\n\nexample (a : Nat) : a + 0 = a := by\n  rwa [Nat.add_zero]\n\n\n`rw` already closes the goal\n\nHint: Use `rw` instead of `rwa`:\n  [apply] rw [Nat.add_zero]\n\nNote: This linter can be disabled with `set_option linter.unnecessaryRwa false`\n\n\nThe forms rwa [rules] at h₁ h₂ and rwa [rules] at * are deprecated; use rw [rules] at h₁ h₂ <;> assumption instead.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.35.0 (2026-10-12)\u0009Highlights\u0009Tactic Changes","header":"A Redesigned  rwa","id":"/releases/v4.35.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___35___0-_LPAR_2026-10-12_RPAR_--Highlights--Tactic-Changes--A-Redesigned--rwa"},"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-_LPAR_2026-11-16_RPAR_--Other":{"contents":"* #16478\nupdates bundled lean-beam in Lean 4.36 with speculative profiling and improvements to Lean language server compatibility, cancellation, and error reporting.* #15665\nadds Lean Beam's CLI, MCP server, daemon, and LSP plugin to Linux and macOS release toolchains.* #15559\nstops a module from exporting constants such as foo.eq_1 when they were realized only inside a proof, so that such proofs no longer trigger downstream rebuilds. Importing modules realize these constants again on demand.* #15558\nstops a module from exporting the entries of declaration-keyed environment extensions, such as projection function info, that belong to private declarations, so that changes to them no longer trigger downstream rebuilds.* #15565\nfixes a panic in leanir and three smaller issues found while enabling compiler.postponeCompile for core.* #15512\nspeeds up module system imports outside the language server and reduces their memory use by no longer building a redundant copy of the imported environment extension entries. Mathlib import time is reduced by ~6%.* #15150\nensures that Lean.toolchain includes a v for release versions, e.g. \"leanprover/lean4:v4.34.0-rc2\", to match the standard format of toolchains in lean-toolchain files. (This also enables ToolchainVer.ofString to succeed on Lean.toolchain.)\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.36.0 (2026-11-16)","header":"Other","id":"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-_LPAR_2026-11-16_RPAR_--Other"},"/releases/v4.37.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___37___0-rc1-_LPAR_2026-11-18_RPAR_--Tactics":{"contents":"* #16532\nmakes simp faster on terms with long projection chains, such as the ones in the proof of the unfold theorem (f.eq_def) of a function defined by structural recursion over a large nested inductive type.* #16507\nmakes vcgen [f] linear in the number of join points of f. Making the spec for the unfold equation of f zeta-reduced its right-hand side, the whole program, which copied each join point into all its jumps. For a chain of 16 ifs that reassign a mutable variable, vcgen +jp [chain] now takes 28 ms instead of 3.2 s.* #16525\nchanges how the grind commutative ring solver picks the basis equation used to simplify a monomial. It now uses the equation with the fewest terms among those whose leading monomial divides the monomial. This keeps intermediate polynomials smaller on some problems. On the ring tests and benchmarks the output is unchanged and the total instruction count drops by 0.5%, with grind_ring_5 9.3% faster.* #16504\nfixes process aborts with INTERNAL PANIC: Nat.pow exponent is too big in simp, grind, cbv, bv_decide and bv_normalize. They happened on goals containing a BitVec literal of width 2^32 or more, such as example (x : BitVec 5000000000) : 1#5000000000 = x := by simp, and on powers with an exponent of 2^32 or more when exponentiation.threshold was raised above 2^32. These terms are now left unevaluated. The examples from #15786 now pass.* #16499\nimproves grind's ring solver on commutative rings that do not implement NoNatZeroDivisors, where it can now derive equalities it could not derive before. Example:example [CommRing α] (x y : α) (h₁ : 2*x = y) (h₂ : 3*x = y + 1) : x = 1 := by\n  grobner\n* #16468\nadds a new tactic called epr for solving EPR problems using a BitVec encoding combined with bv_decide. This tactic will be particularly useful for applications in protocol verifiers such as Veil.* #16456\nimplements Z3-style instantiation sets for MBQI based model-checking.* #16455\nintroduces a Paradox-style union-find based subsort analysis to bv_decide's EPR support. It operates in two steps:1. Infer subsorts using a normal union-find algorithm and at the same time detect (non)-monotone sorts using the syntactic positive equality criterion.2. For all monotone sorts that have more than one syntactic subsort do split them up into actual subsorts in the EPR problem.* #16453\nimplements some symmetry breaking for the EPR encoding used in bv_decide, we provide 3 different modes:* off, no symmetry breaking, provide the minimum sound encoding. That is, every sort element gets assigned a  BitVec variable that is unconstrained besides the maximum sort cardinality.* simple, the default. Instead of leaving them unconstrained we say that the bitvec encoding of element a_i must be <= i. This is similar to one of Paradox's symmetry breakers introduced in \"New Techniques that Improve MACE-style Finite Model Finding\"* full. This introduces as much symmetry breaking as possible, in addition to simple this introduces the fact that if a_i != i then all a_j with j > i will also have a_j != i.* #16452\nintroduces a basic EPR solver to bv_decide. It detects type parameters that act like EPR sorts, encodes them into a BitVec representation and finally solves the problem using a naive MBQI loop. Follow up work will provide a convenient top level tactic for this.* #16484\nmakes vcgen +jp state the equation of a join-point parameter once, above the disjunction over the jumps, if every jump passes the same term for it. For a join point that many jumps reach in the same state t, the join hypothesis is now s = t ∧ (d₁ ∨ … ∨ dₙ), so each disjunct dᵢ has one equation fewer.* #16414\nfixes a stack overflow in sym => when apply unifies a metavariable with a term that contains it. For example, apply Eq.refl on the goal ?n = ?n * ?n used to assign ?n := ?n * ?n, and now fails with \"rule is not applicable\".* #16346\nadds the config options simpMaxSteps and simpMaxDischargeDepth to vcgen. They set maxSteps and maxDischargeDepth of every Sym.simp run of the simp step, as the fields of a register_sym_simp variant do. The defaults are those of Sym.Simp.Config.* #16316\nimproves cbv handling of match equations. Previously cbv, when encountering match expressions would try first to use matcher equations using Sym machinery for theorem rewrite, with no discharger enabled, before falling back to whnf. Now, we try a discharger that can deal with overlap hypotheses, by applying cbv to each of the arguments of the overlaph hypothesis. Literals nested inside constructor pattterns, still fall back to whnf, since Sym.simp has no simproc  for constructor injectivity yet.* #16341\nfixes the grind internal error \"linarith term has not been internalized\" on negative numerals such as -1 : T, where T unfolds to Int and has an ordered IntModule instance but no CommRing instance.* #16319\nchanges the proofs produced by lia, grind, and the Nat offset simprocs so that the kernel no longer has to evaluate their arithmetic atoms. Before, for goals containing terms such as (x + 4294967295) % 4294967296, the kernel ran in unary Nat arithmetic and failed to check the proofs in practice. Closes #16311.* #16310\nmakes vcgen keep its goals maximally shared, and runs the vcgen and mvcgen tests with sym.debug set, so the test suite catches a change that breaks a SymM invariant.* #16291\nadds the attribute @[vcgen_rintro], which tells vcgen how to destructure a binder while it introduces the binders of a goal, like a fixed rintro pattern. For example, with* #16303\nmakes vcgen keep the value of a let or have in the program as written, as before #16292, so that vcgen +jp is as fast as before. For example, have x := 0 + k; pure (x + x) leaves x : Nat := 0 + k in the VC under vcgen simp [Nat.zero_add].* #16292\nmakes vcgen step past a let or have at the head of the right-hand side of an entailment pre ⊑ rhs. A spec such as* #16298\nfixes grind linarith failing with the internal error \"structure is not a preorder\" on ≤ and < facts over types with an order but no OrderedAdd instance, such as Fin n and BitVec w. For example, (x : Fin 3) (h : x < x) : False is now proved by grind_linarith, and the satisfiable (x y : Fin 3) (h₁ : x < y) (h₂ : y + 1 < x + 1) : False now fails with a regular grind failure message.* #16297\nfixes a kernel type mismatch on eq_of_dvd_ediv in proofs produced by lia and grind, for goals such as x % 3 ≤ 2 * x % 3 → 4 * x % 3 ≤ 2 * x % 3.* #16294\nfixes the internal error mkNoConfusion: No manifest constructors in contradiction on hypotheses such as h : 0 + 0 = 1.* #16261\nmakes vcgen report an error when the goal is a Std.Do triple or entailment, and when Std.WP is not imported. The error message comes with a hint what to do. The Std.Do error asks the user to restate the goal with the definitions from Std.WP, and to add import Std.WP when it is missing.* #16280\nmakes vcgen rewrite a match on a string literal discriminant in its verification conditions, e.g. match \"done\" with | \"start\" => False | \"done\" => True | _ => False reduces to True.* #16264\nfixes a kernel error in the Sym.simp-based arithmetic normalizer on types of characteristic 1, such as Fin 1. The normalizer now leaves these types as written.* #16263\nfixes a grind.debug panic in checkEqc on goals such as x = 1 → -x = -1 for Int8, Int16, Int32, and Int64.* #16226\nchanges bv_normalize to explicitly eliminate BitVec.cast via equational rewriting with BitVec.cast_eq as opposed to rfl. Michael Tautschnig noticed that eliminating via rfl forces the kernel to evaluate the term being cast which is potentially expensive.* #16181\nchanges vcgen so that reduceHead? substitutes an assigned metavariable at the head of a term and beta-reduces, and instantiateGoal? reduces the head of the entailment with it, so a goal whose right-hand side holds unassigned metavariables below the head is no longer instantiated in full at every step.\nAnother consequence is that a match postcondition applied to a constructor yields one verification condition per conjunct, which renumbers the case vcN tags of such goals.* #16241\nfixes sym => tactics that failed or made no progress on goals mentioning metavariables assigned after the goal was created, e.g., by apply or by solving a sibling goal. This affected intro, apply, simp, dsimp, let_to_have, lift_lets, and local instance registration.* #16222\nuses a CaDiCaL terminator based on a Lean BaseIO Bool callback for incremental solves instead of spawning a second thread that kills the solving process if it takes too long.* #16069\nintroduces a generic incremental bitvector solver into bv_decide. For now this is only used to feed the BitVec component of the CEGAR loop but it will have additional applications in the future.* #16212\nfixes vcgen +jp for a join point nested in a branch of another join point. Before, such a program failed with \"vcgen +jp: unexpected binder\" or \"vcgen +jp: missing witness\".* #16221\ndisables CaDiCaL's lucky pass in incremental mode. At the beginning of each incremental solve it visits the whole problem trying an all true/all false assignment. This is quadratic if we sequence many incremental solves.* #16102\nadds the lattice simplifications of simp and grind norm to the @[vcgen_simp] set, so vcgen states verification conditions at carrier Prop with ∧, ∨, ∃, ∀ and → in place of ⊓, ⊔, ⨆, ⨅ and ⊑. The set also eliminates the index of a supremum when an equation determines it. For example, from the precondition fun s => s = 3, the postcondition fun _ s => ⨆ n : Nat, ⌜s = n⌝ ⊓ ⌜n < 5⌝ leaves no verification condition, and the postcondition fun _ _ => ⨆ n : Nat, ⌜n < 5⌝ leaves ∃ n, n < 5.* #16216\nmakes the join point hypotheses of vcgen +jp smaller. For two sequential ifs that modify the state, the hypothesis of the second join point is now b 0 = true ∧ s = s₀ + 1 ∨ ¬b 0 = true ∧ s = s₀ + 2. Before, it also contained the equation x = (PUnit.unit, s₀ + 1).fst for the result of the first if.* #16192\nmakes vcgen instantiate assigned metavariables in hypothesis types before introducing them to the local context. This results in more Sym term sharing and more reliable vcgen operation in general.* #16207\nfixes a panic and a kernel error in grind with the new normalizer (backward.grind.normalizer false) when the local context contains a let-variable, as in (h : a * b = c) : let x := a * b; x = c after intro x.* #16204\nfixes grind failing on goals that mix a cast from Nat and a cast from Int into the same ring, such as (m : Int) (k : Nat) (h : m = k) : (m : R) = (k : R). With the new normalizer (backward.grind.normalizer false) this also affected goals written as (m : R) = ((k : Int) : R), because the normalizer rewrites ((k : Int) : R) to (k : R).* #16203\nmakes Sym handle let and have declarations. Backward rules (sym => apply, vcgen specs and unfold rules) now keep the haves of their hypotheses in the new goals instead of copying the values into every use. Matching and unification now unfold lets and haves in the goal instead of failing or reporting \"unexpected let-declaration term\". As a result, vcgen +jp [f.eq_def] shares the join points of f: with n sequential ifs it produces 2 goals instead of 2ⁿ⁺¹ (part of #16122). A have in the program can now appear in a verification condition as a let-variable.* #16199\nintroduces a cache for bitblasting of boolean substructure in bv_decide. Note that at the bit/AIG level this was already being cached but we never the less repeatedly traversed the expressions before. This has so far not been an issue as our problems mostly consisted of BitVec expressions which is not necessarily the case anymore.* #16193\nfixes a kernel error in grind on match expressions with overlapping patterns where a literal in the discriminant meets a pattern variable, e.g. p (3, false) with p defined by match s with | (_, true) => 0 | _ => 1.* #16167\nfixes grind failing to prove (m : R) = (k : R) for a ring R such as ZMod n when the facts force m = k but lia does not derive it, e.g., from m ≤ k, k ≤ m + 1 and 2 ∣ k - m. This fixes a Mathlib regression introduced by #16161.* #16165\nmakes the Sym.Arith normalizer push Nat and Int casts through +, * and powers with a literal exponent, and for Int also through -, negation and casts from Nat. As a result, (m + n) • x = m • x + n • x and ((m * n : Nat) : R) = ((n * m : Nat) : R) are now proved by Sym.simp with arith, and by the grind normalizer. Truncated Nat subtraction, division and remainder are still treated as atoms.* #16164\nfixes sym => dsimp timing out on large, deeply nested terms that sym => simp handles quickly.* #16163\nmakes Sym.simp reduce a match whose discriminant is a value such as a String, Nat or Char literal, e.g. match \"b\" with | \"a\" => 1 | \"b\" => 2 | _ => 3. This affects sym => simp and the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer, which previously left such terms unchanged.* #16162\nfixes Sym pattern matching (used by Sym.simp, Sym.dsimp and apply in sym mode) for theorems whose universe variables first occur inside a max level, such as rewriting @id (α × β) p with id_prod {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (p : α × β) : @id (α × β) p = p when β : Type (max v w). Previously, the matcher guessed the universe variables by splitting the max argument by argument, and failed when a later occurrence such as Prod.{u, v} required different values.* #16161\nfixes grind model-based theory combination making useless case splits on x = y for Nat terms such as 3 * x, x / 3 and x % 3, which it treated as nonlinear because only Int numerals were recognized.* #16159\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer handle integer powers with a literal exponent in a field. For example, 2 ^ (-1 : Int) becomes 2⁻¹ and a ^ (2 : Int) becomes a ^ 2, so they take part in the polynomial normal form. The legacy normalizer only evaluates a numeral power inside ⁻¹ or /.* #16158\nfixes universe level unification in Sym, which could assign a universe level metavariable to a level containing itself (causing a stack overflow) or overwrite an existing assignment (leaving universe metavariables in the proof).* #16157\nfixes grind with the Sym.simp normalizer on goals that use / in a field of nonzero characteristic. For example, a / c = b → b = a * c⁻¹ over [Field α] [IsCharP α 7] now closes, as it does with the legacy normalizer.* #16156\nmakes the Sym.simp arithmetic normalizer, used by sym => simp and the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer, prove ground Nat, Int and Rat subterms such as 2 ^ 64 or the 2 + 3 in a + (2 + 3) by rfl instead of a ring-reflection certificate, which reduces kernel checking time.* #16152\nreduces the fixed cost of every grind and lia call by about 25 µs.* #16147\nreplaces the grind option splits with branches, which limits the total number of branches that case splitting may create during a proof search (default 1024). Previously, grind, lia, and the related tactics allowed at most 9 case splits along each branch of the search, so a goal with a long chain of cheap case splits failed even when its proof was tiny. For example, a hypothesis x ≤ 0 ∨ 100 ≤ x ∨ x = 2 ∨ x = 4 ∨ … ∨ x = 36 whose disjuncts are each refuted immediately needed 17 case splits in a row and exceeded the limit. Such goals are now proved with the default configuration, while the total amount of case splitting stays bounded.* #16143\nmakes lia (and grind) assert the linear bounds that hold in every disjunct of a disjunction, instead of finding them by case splitting. For example, (b = true ∧ y = x + 1) ∨ (b = false ∧ y = x + 2) implies x + 1 ≤ y and y ≤ x + 2. Goals produced by vcgen +jp (#16122) carry one such disjunction per join point. On them, lia explored all 2ⁿ cases and failed for n ≥ 10 at the splits threshold. It now solves them in time linear in n.* #16141\nfixes an exponential slowdown in grind on terms whose types are equal only modulo the E-graph, such as Fin (a.set! i v).size and Fin a.size, or the bounds proofs j < (a.set! i v).size and j < a.size in array accesses.* #16131\nmakes rw and other tactics based on kabstract faster on goals with shared subterms.* #16130\nmakes rw faster on large goals by type checking only the parts of the motive that depend on the rewritten occurrences.* #16129\nspeeds up grind on goals with many nested case splits. Building the proof of a case split no longer traverses the proofs of the case splits below it.* #16124\nfixes a kernel \"loose bound variables\" error in sym => when two have or let expressions differ only in their type.* #16118\nadds support for propositional variables in bv_decide even when they are not wrapped in decide p = true.* #16106\nmakes vcgen discharge the hypotheses of conditional theorems in its simp set. The simp set (@[vcgen_simp] and vcgen simp [...]) now rewrites with the discharger Sym.Simp.dischargeSimpSelf.\nExample: Mem.loadInt_storeInt (hw : n ≤ 2 ^ w) : (m.storeInt a n v).loadInt a n = some (Int.ofBytes (Int.toBytes n v)). At a call site n and w are literals, and ground evaluation proves 8 ≤ 2 ^ 64.* #16114\nfixes an unknown free variable error when lia closes a subgoal of cases_next in grind interactive mode, e.g., grind => cases_next; all_goals finish.* #16112\nfixes an internal grind error (\"term has not been internalized\") caused by structure eta.* #16094\nmakes vcgen leave out a lifted equation x = t after it substitutes t for x, when no other hypothesis mentions x. The equation then carries no information, and the verification conditions no longer show it. For example, the verification condition of a program that throws \"x\" against the postcondition fun e => e = \"x\" ∨ e = \"y\" was* #16093\nlets a @[frameproc] name a theorem framesThm? that proves WP.Frames op x F P for every program x, frame F and guard P. vcgen discharges the frame condition of each framed call with that theorem, so the frame condition no longer becomes a verification condition.* #16092\nmakes try? treat a candidate tactic that exceeds the maximum recursion depth as having failed, as it already did for the heartbeat limit, instead of aborting with that error. For example, impossible by decide on a goal quantifying over UInt64 hits the recursion limit and previously made the whole try? call fail.* #16085\nreduces the time grind spends in its order solver when many terms are related by fixed offsets, such as numerals in an ordered ring. Adding n numerals used to take time cubic in n and now takes quadratic time. For example, a goal with 300 hypotheses yₖ = k over Int now takes 0.44 s instead of 1.86 s.* #16075\nmakes the grind failure message reported without +verbose list the thresholds that were reached, as configuration items the user can raise. For example, lia now fails with `lia` has failed after reaching the threshold `(splits := 9)`, use `+verbose` to obtain diagnostic information. This applies to instances, ematch, splits, gen, ringSteps, and liaSteps, and to all tactics built on grind, such as lia, grind_order, grind_linarith, and grobner.* #16073\nshortens the error message reported when grind fails without +verbose. Instead of printing the failing goal, the message is now `<tactic>` has failed, use `+verbose` to obtain diagnostic information, where <tactic> is the tactic the user invoked (e.g., lia, grind_order, grind_linarith, grobner, sym) rather than always grind. With +verbose, the goal and diagnostics are still reported, and the header also names the invoked tactic.* #16072\nfixes a grind panic in mkCongrDefaultProof when using Function.Injective hypotheses together with case splits (#16059).* #16056\nfixes grind? and finish? dropping case-splits from their generated scripts when the proof does not use the case-split, but the rest of the script does. For example, a case-split may introduce a term that E-matching uses later. The scripts then failed to replay, and grind? dropped theorems from its grind only suggestions.* #16050\nfixes grind? generating a · done step for a case-split alternative that grind closes while introducing its hypotheses. The step failed when the script was replayed, which also made grind? drop theorems from its grind only suggestions.* #16057\nmakes grind? check each suggested tactic before showing it. Previously, only the generated grind => script was checked, and it was suggested even when the check failed. Suggestions that do not close the goal are now reported in a warning instead of being suggested, and if none of them works, grind? suggests grind with the original configuration and parameters. try? uses the same check for the grind only suggestions it shows.* #16054\nfixes grind? suggesting grind only [...] calls that fail because they need more E-matching rounds than the ematch limit allows. The search that found the proof may have performed more rounds because case-splits reset the round counter, even when those case-splits were not needed by the proof and were removed from the generated script. The suggestions now set the ematch limit, e.g., grind (ematch := 6) only [...], when the generated script performs more rounds between case-splits than the limit.* #16052\nfixes instantiate only [!thm] ignoring the ! modifier when thm is already registered as a grind theorem, and using its registered patterns instead of the minimal indexable ones. Generated scripts using !thm then failed to replay, and grind? dropped thm from its grind only suggestions.* #16051\nfixes grind? and finish? dropping solver steps such as ring and lia that make progress without closing the goal or propagating facts. Later steps may depend on that progress, so the generated script failed to replay, and grind? also dropped theorems from its grind only suggestions.* #16048\nfixes grind? producing scripts that pick the wrong case-split when local variables are shadowed, which also made it drop theorems from its grind only suggestions.* #16042\nfixes grind? dropping [grind inj] theorems from its grind only suggestions, which then failed when pasted.* #16041\nfixes anchors such as #c0c6 in grind only [...] being parsed as terms when a # prefix notation is in scope, which made grind? suggestions fail. A # notation applied to a hexadecimal identifier can still be written with parentheses, e.g. grind only [(#ab)].* #16040\nfixes instantiate only [...] never firing instances of theorems registered with grind_pattern ... where guard ..., which made grind? drop such theorems from its suggestions.* #15961\nmakes vcgen simplify the verification conditions it produces, together with the hypotheses and program states along the way. By default, it uses the theorems tagged @[vcgen_simp] and evaluates ground terms such as 41 + 1, so that (a, b).snd = 7 becomes b = 7 and a branch whose condition evaluates to False closes at once. vcgen simp [thm, h, *] adds theorems for one call and replaces simplifying_assumptions [...]. vcgen -simp turns simplification off.* #15999\nmakes kernel checking of Sym.simp's arithmetic normalization and of grind's ring solver proofs much cheaper over few variables. Polynomial identities are checked by evaluating both sides at one point, instead of normalizing them in the kernel.* #15995\nmakes kernel checking of Sym.simp's arithmetic normal forms and identities, and of grind's Gröbner chains, cheaper. It uses polynomials whose monomials are packed into natural numbers (Grind.CommRing.PPoly, in the new file Init/Grind/Ring/Packed.lean).* #15990\nmakes kernel checking of Sym.simp arithmetic normalization much cheaper for terms with large products, such as (a + b + c + d + e) ^ 7 = (a + b + c + d + e) ^ 3 * (a + b + c + d + e) ^ 4.* #15988\nmakes kernel checking of Sym.simp arithmetic relations and identities cheaper.* #15986\nmakes kernel checking of Sym.simp arithmetic normalization much cheaper for long sums and large polynomials.* #15984\nspeeds up kernel checking of the certificates produced by the arith simproc of Sym.simp, and adds benchmarks for the simproc.* #15983\nmakes kernel checking of cutsat proofs and of the simp +arith steps that grind uses to normalize hypotheses cheaper. On the lia benchmarks, kernel checking becomes 38–46% cheaper.* #15981\nmakes kernel checking of grind's semiring, field and linarith proofs cheaper. It replaces definitions that the kernel reduces through brecOn, structural recursion or derived BEq instances with recursor-based ones.* #15967\nmakes lia, grind and Sym.simp's arith simproc cheaper by answering the algebraic structure queries of Sym.Arith classification for Int and Nat from a table, without type class resolution. Each grind goal classified these types again, issuing about 20 queries that the instance cache did not carry over between goals.* #15970\nmakes kernel checking of grind ring proofs much cheaper, especially for long Gröbner basis derivations.* #15969\nmakes grind ring proofs smaller by building the expression of each distinct monomial and coefficient once.* #15944\nspeeds up kernel checking of ring and field certificates produced by grind and Sym.simp.* #15943\nmakes the kernel check the certificates of the grind ring solver and of the Sym.simp arithmetic normalizer about twice as fast. It also adds benchmarks for them under tests/ring_perf.* #15934\noptimizes literal recognition and improves grind support for rational and signed fixed-width integer literals.* #15926\nmakes grind and its variants such as lia invoke only the solvers that the configuration enables. Each solver extension declares when it is enabled, and grind computes the active solvers once per run, instead of calling every solver and letting each one check its own flag.* #15924\nspeeds up lia on goals that contain a / k or a % k for a numeral k.* #15922\nmakes grind and lia use the core instances for OfNat Int n, OfNat Nat n, and Decidable of ≤, <, and = over Int and Nat without running type class resolution when canonicalizing terms.* #15917\nmakes the kernel check the remaining lia certificates faster, including those for divisibility constraints, Cooper resolution, and constant folding. They now evaluate recursor-based versions of the operations they still unfolded through match, decide, or fuel. Theorems that take user hypotheses now state them with Expr.denote_k, so matching a hypothesis no longer unfolds Expr.denote. Init.Data.Int.Kernel gains versions of -, /, %, ^, natAbs, toNat, gcd, lcm, and bmod.* #15914\nmakes lia add the linear facts it derives for a / k and a % k without running them through the simp-based preprocessor. These facts are linear combinations that lia normalizes itself.* #15912\nmakes grind's markNestedSubsingletons preprocessing step reuse the core cache for instantiated constant types instead of instantiating the type of every application head again.* #15913\nfixes grind ignoring hypotheses such as ∀ n, v = f n → False, where f n is not a pattern. The equivalent ∀ n, ¬ v = f n worked.* #15911\nmakes the kernel check lia certificates faster. Certificates now evaluate versions of polynomial normalization and Int arithmetic written with recursors, so the kernel no longer unfolds match and structural recursion. The Int versions live in the new module Init.Data.Int.Kernel for reuse.* #15910\nmakes the Nat and Int linear-constraint parsers used by simp_arith, grind, and lia check that a comparison is over Nat (resp. Int) before testing its instance. Before, Simp.Arith.Nat.ToLinear.toLinearCnstr? matched LE.le _ i a b and called DefEq.isInstLENat i on every comparison, so each Int comparison paid a failing isDefEqI against instLENat; Simp.Arith.Int.ToLinear.leCnstr? and dvdCnstr? had the same problem with Nat comparisons.* #15909\nfixes grind missing a match-expression condition when a constructor for a nested subterm arrives after the outer merge. For example, given h : ∀ n, v = .lit (.nat n) → False, grind closed the goal from l = .nat m and v = .lit l only when the facts arrived in that order. The same issue prevented conditions of match equations from becoming true, e.g., f v = 2 for f with the alternatives .lit (.nat _) => 1 | _ => 2, given v = .lit l and l = .str s from a case split.* #15906\nchanges the default of the grind option verbose to false, which also applies to lia, cutsat, grind_linarith, grind_order, and grobner. Failures now show only the remaining goal and the hint use +verbose to obtain diagnostic information. Collecting and printing diagnostics took about 38% of the time spent on easy failing lia goals.* #15905\nmakes lia (cutsat) prove a constraint once, in the goal where it was stored, instead of once in every branch below it. On goals with many grind case splits, lia is up to 2.8x faster in elaboration and up to 200x faster in the kernel.* #15891\nfixes a panic in vcgen under set_option sym.debug true. The panic occurred whenever vcgen substituted a precondition x = t.* #15884\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer (backward.grind.normalizer := false) cancel the common part of both sides of a Nat subtraction, as the legacy normalizer does with Nat.reduceSubDiff: n + 1 - 1 becomes n, n + 2 - 1 becomes n + 1, and 3 - (n + 1) becomes 2 - n. Common monomials are cancelled too, so n + k - k becomes n and x * y + 3 - (x * y + z + 1) becomes 2 - z.* #15874\nfixes a grind.debug invariant violation in checkEqc on goals such as (p && p && q) = (p && q).* #15873\nfixes a grind.debug internal error on ground Rat arithmetic such as (3 : Rat) * 2 = 6.* #15872\nfixes grind producing a proof the kernel rejects (decide (1 = 1) = false) when the same literal occurs at definitionally equal but syntactically different types, such as (1 : BitVec w) and (1 : BitVec 32) for @[implicit_reducible] def w : Nat := 32.* #15862\nfixes the internal grind error \"term has not been internalized\" on an HEq fact whose sides are proofs of BitVec, UInt8, or Fin equations, e.g., p ≍ True.intro with p : a = 1. Such facts are also created by grind when it splits a match with a proof discriminant.* #15860\nfixes a kernel error in grind when a BitVec width or Fin bound is a definition, e.g., x : BitVec W with def W : Nat := 4.* #15854\nfixes grind? suggesting a grind only [...] call that drops a premise used in the proof, together with a generated tactic script that fails to close the goal. This happened when the goal contains several case-split candidates that differ only in hidden variables, such as the w✝s introduced by function extensionality.* #15852\nfixes a kernel type error in proofs produced by grind when goals contain heterogeneous arithmetic applications such as c * xs with c : Nat and xs : Vector Nat n.* #15851\nfixes a stack overflow in grind when a match-expression condition refers to a term in a cyclic equivalence class, such as as = a :: as.* #15850\nfixes a grind proof that the kernel rejected when the goal involves a ring of characteristic 1, such as Fin 1. Disequalities in such rings now close the goal directly.* #15847\nfixes the grind.debug internal error \"internalize reached an application whose arguments are still being internalized\" on casts such as ((n - m : Nat) : Int).* #7942\nupdates to Cadical 3.0.1. While mixed on our checked in benchmarks, on SMT-LIB this was measured to be a general net-positive: config                 status  total  solved    sat  unsat   best  timeout  memout  error  uniq  dis   time_cpu      memory\n no_kernel_new_cadical  ee      46191   45195  17882  27313  17892      595     395      6     0    0  1077161.8  17933756.1\n kernel_new_cadical     ee      46191   45159  17890  27269   5220      642     380     10     7    0  1245494.4  27242333.1\n no_kernel_old_cadical  ee      46191   45141  17827  27314  18892      664     381      5    11    0  1187605.0  18032921.3\n kernel_old_cadical     ee      46191   45097  17833  27264   3950      715     367     12     3    0  1366219.3  27300968.9\n\n* #15838\nmakes lia eliminate equations whose coefficients are all non-unit as in the Omega test, instead of a solved form scaled by the smallest coefficient. Chains of such equations, as produced by / and % definitions, no longer yield bounds with coefficients in the thousands and Cooper splits with as many cases.* #15837\nfixes a panic in the lia model search when a bound mentions a variable created after the first case split: the bound tightening indexed the divisibility constraints of the level-0 snapshot with that variable.* #15836\nfixes an internal grind error (mkEqProof invoked with terms of different types) and missed solver equalities in equivalence classes merged via HEq (e.g., Fin n and Fin m terms related by cast).* #15835\nmakes lia reorder its variables instead of enumerating a Cooper case split with many cases, which fixes goals such as a three-limb counter increment that previously needed a 2^32-way split.* #15833\nfixes a regression from #15827 where lia enumerated all cases of a Cooper split although the untightened bounds refuted every case at once.* #15832\nmakes liaSteps also bound the cases enumerated by Cooper and disequality splits in lia, so that a long enumeration stops at the threshold instead of producing a proof the kernel rejects.* #15831\nfixes a panic and infinite loop in grind's lia model search.* #15830\nfixes a kernel error and missed proofs in lia when an integer term or inequality occurs in a dependent position, where the grind preprocessor cannot normalize it.* #15829\nfixes grind kernel errors and internal errors when proving equalities between terms that contain binders, such as the preconditions of a function unfolded with grind [f].* #15827\nmakes lia solve goals with large-modulus divisibility constraints, such as 65536 ∣ 10 * x ⊢ 32768 ∣ x, without a Cooper case split per residue.* #15826\nfixes lia exhausting liaSteps on goals where its rational model search looped on a divisibility constraint.* #15825\nfixes grind producing a proof the kernel rejects (decide (4 = 4) = false) when a numeral is written as OfNat.ofNat 4.* #15823\nfixes grind producing proofs that the kernel rejects when the same Float or Float32 value is written in different ways, such as 2 and 2.0.* #14398\nimplements vcgen +jp, which verifies the code after an if or a match once instead of once per branch. Without +jp, vcgen copies that code into every branch, so k consecutive splits give 2ᵏ copies of it and its VCs. With +jp, VC generation grows linearly in k. The VCs of the shared code receive a hypothesis that states what each branch knows at its end, for example (n > 0 ∧ x = 1) ∨ (¬n > 0 ∧ x = 2).* #15819\nfixes a kernel error in grind when it combines equations between constant functions and an equivalence class contains more than one lambda.* #15812\nfixes lia failures on goals that mix / and % by the same power of two, such as t % 64 ≤ (64 * t + y) % 4096 / 64, where omega succeeds and lia exceeded liaSteps.* #15804\nadds the grind option maxChar (default 0). The Sym.simp-based normalizer now puts arithmetic terms in normal form only over types whose characteristic is unknown or at most maxChar, so terms over BitVec, Fin n, UIntN, and IntN are left as written by default, as the legacy normalizer does. Before, x - y over BitVec 64 became x + 18446744073709551615 * y, which hid the subtraction from [grind =] rules keyed on x - y and from the translation of BitVec/UIntN terms into Nat.* #15803\nfixes a grind incompleteness where a lambda that occurs as an argument of two different functions never gets a function extensionality case split for the second one. For example, f (fun x => k x a) = f (fun x => k x b) with a = b now closes even when fun x => k x a also occurs in a hypothesis g (fun x => k x a).* #15802\nmakes grind learn p v from a term ⟨v, h⟩ : {x // p x}, so facts about subtypes are usable even when the type of the stored proof h is only definitionally equal to p v. For example, after Subtype.forall normalizes ∀ x : {b // R a b}, q x to ∀ b (h : R a b), q ⟨b, h⟩, the instance for ⟨b, hab⟩ with hab : diag R a b is now discharged.* #15796\nmakes vcgen substitute a pure precondition x = t on a local variable x into the rest of the goal. The hypothesis x = t stays in the local context. For example, if the postcondition of a call let r ← g says r = 41, then a spec for f 41 applies to the next call f r. Verification conditions now show t where they showed x before. This feature is important to support for Iris' HeapLang example.* #15773\nfixes a maxRecDepth failure of grind with the Sym.simp normalizer on hypotheses such as decide (rc ≤ Int32.minValue + c) = false, and makes the normalizer produce the standard instance after rewriting under decide.* #15772\nmakes grind with the Sym.simp normalizer evaluate USize.toNat n for a numeral n < 2^32, so goals such as 0 < (0x10000 : USize).toNat are closed as they are with the legacy normalizer.* #15770\nfixes grind failures with the Sym.simp normalizer on hypotheses such as (let j := k; fun s => s.2 = j) (a, b), a shape produced by mvcgen frames.* #15767\nfixes a kernel error of grind with the Sym.simp normalizer on goals such as i ≠ 0 for i : BitVec (id 32).* #15766\nfixes a kernel error of grind with the Sym.simp normalizer on hypotheses such as a ≠ 1/2 over a field of characteristic zero.* #15765\nfixes a kernel error of grind with the Sym.simp normalizer on goals such as z / (-0) = 0 over a field.* #15764\nmakes simp +arith report no change on an Int term that is already in normal form, and makes both grind normalizers keep an equation between a numeral and an atom in the orientation it was written, also when the numeral side is an expression such as 2 * 3 - 7 or (3 : Fin 5) + 4.* #15763\nmakes simp +arith and both grind normalizers write the equations x + -1 * y = 0 and x + k = 0 as x = y and x = -k. These were the documented normal forms, but inputs already of the shape p = 0 were left alone, so x + 5 = 0 and x + -1 * y = 0 stayed as they were while 2 * x = 2 * y became x = y.* #15760\nfixes a simp loop on a dependent ∀ (h : p), q h with p : Prop when simplifying p returns a proof but leaves the term unchanged. This happens, for example, with simp +arith when p contains Int arithmetic that is already in normal form, and it made grind and lia hit maxRecDepth on hypotheses such as ∀ (k : ℕ) (x : X k), … after Subtype.forall became a grind normalization rule.* #15753\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer (backward.grind.normalizer := false) eta-reduce the lambda arguments of an application before their bodies are simplified, so id (fun x => Nat.succ x) = id Nat.succ closes: the lambda becomes Nat.succ instead of fun x => x + 1, which nothing could relate to the unapplied Nat.succ. The legacy normalizer closed such goals through eq_self up to definitional equality, which Sym.simp does not use.* #15725\nremoves the rebuild of the Sym.simp normalization theorem set on every pattern normalization in grind. With backward.grind.normalizer := false, every [grind] attribute application and every theorem referenced by instantiate/finish paid a full conversion of the [grind norm] set (grind_ite_trace timed out).* #15748\nadds Subtype.forall and Subtype.exists to the grind normalization rules, so a hypothesis ∀ α : {a // p a}, q α becomes ∀ a, p a → q ⟨a, _⟩. Since grind splits subtype binders eagerly, the goal never contains Subtype.val terms, and the original E-matching pattern Subtype.val ?α of such a hypothesis could not be instantiated.* #15745\nfixes grind not introducing the hypotheses of an implication or universal quantifier when the goal type is wrapped in Expr.mdata, as it is after a have (noImplicitLambda). The goal was negated as a whole instead, so a hypothesis ¬(p → q) reached the normalizer and the solvers.* #15703\nfixes a grind failure with the Sym.simp normalizer (backward.grind.normalizer false) on goals such as the one in grind_9216: a negated existential ¬∃ a, (p a ∨ q a) ∧ ∃ b, r a b must become two universally quantified facts, one per disjunct, so that each gets its own E-matching pattern. Sym.simp left it as a single ∀ a b, … with a disjunction in the body.* #15701\nfixes a grind failure, \"error while initializing arithmetic operators\", triggered by the Sym.simp normalizer (backward.grind.normalizer false) on a semiring whose synthesized NatCast instance is not reducibly defeq to the natCast field of its Grind.Semiring instance, even when the goal contains no casts.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.37.0-rc1 (2026-11-18)","header":"Tactics","id":"/releases/v4.37.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___37___0-rc1-_LPAR_2026-11-18_RPAR_--Tactics"}});